window.RDG={"img":"","img_index":1,"img_label":"Pullover","lab":{"example":"Every small model here trains on <b>Fashion-MNIST</b>: 70,000 grey 28 &times; 28 images of clothing in 10 classes (MIT licence, Zalando), padded to 32 &times; 32; clothing shows blur, dropped modes and class confusions by eye, and a 1M-parameter U-Net learns it in minutes on a laptop GPU. Sixteen fixed test images are used for every before/after. The pretrained models, pinned to exact revisions, are <b>Stable Diffusion v1.5</b> (CreativeML OpenRAIL-M), <b>CLIP ViT-B/32</b>, <b>SigLIP 2 base</b> and the VLM <b>SmolVLM-500M-Instruct</b> (both Apache 2.0), run on sixteen colour test images with known answers (photographs in the public domain or CC0, synthetic scenes, Stable Diffusion outputs). Details and licences: the root's <code>src/data/README.md</code>.","sd_params":"Counted from the pinned v1.5 weights: U-Net 859,520,964, VAE 83,653,863, text encoder 123,060,480 parameters <span class=\"meas\">measured</span> (setup, <code>src/data/smoke/sd.json</code>), matching the README's 860M and 123M.","sdvae":"Over the sixteen colour test images: photographs 26.7 to 30.9 dB PSNR, synthetic graphics 32.3 to 37.5 dB, a generated puppy's fur 20.6 dB; fp16 within 0.1 dB of fp32. The raw latent's spread over all sixteen is 5.51, so 1 / std = 0.1816, 0.3% below 0.18215: the factor is \"divide by the latent's spread\" <span class=\"meas\">measured</span> (Latent lab).","sd_time":"Stable Diffusion v1.5, 512 &times; 512, 25 guided DPM-Solver steps, fp16 with attention slicing: 15.75 s warm (0.610 s per guided step against 0.319 s for one pass; decode 0.488 s; text encoder 0.025 s), peak 4,452 MiB <span class=\"meas\">measured</span> on Apple M1 Pro (Diffusion lab; peak from the setup). A datacenter GPU is many times faster per step; the step count is the same.","clip":"Zero-shot on the 10,000 Fashion-MNIST test images: CLIP B/32 66.7% at best, SigLIP 2 74.5% (chance 10%); a linear probe on 16 labelled images per class: 79.0% and 84.0%. SigLIP 2 drops to about 22% if class names keep their capitals (its reference code lowercases text). Typographic probe: CLIP prefers \"cat\" for the cat photo (cosine 0.2846 against 0.2318) and flips to \"dog\" with a <code>DOG</code> sticker (0.2775 against 0.2855); SigLIP 2 does not (0.1282 against 0.1112) <span class=\"meas\">measured</span> (Vision lab).","vlm_tokens":"SmolVLM-500M turns a 512 &times; 512 image into <b>1,088 image tokens</b>: scaled to 2,048 pixels, cut into 16 tiles plus one whole view, 64 tokens each after a pixel shuffle; with a short question the prompt is 1,142 tokens. Input resolution does not change the count (a 64-pixel square also gives 1,088); aspect ratio and the long-side setting do (512 &times; 256: 576; one tile: 64; six per side: 2,368) <span class=\"meas\">measured</span> (VLM lab).","fid_n":"On this page's data, real Fashion-MNIST training images scored against the 10,000 real test images (so nothing is wrong) give FID 2.54 at 10,000 images, 3.84 at 5,000, 15.4 to 15.8 at 1,000, 27.9 to 28.1 at 500 and 44.9 to 47.4 at 250 <span class=\"meas\">measured</span> (setup, <code>fid_floor.json</code>, three draws below 10,000).","ae_prior":"16-number codes, 1,000 random Gaussian codes decoded: plain autoencoder FD 1,756 with 96% of samples classified \"Bag\", VAE (&beta; = 1) 689, and the autoencoder with a Gaussian fitted to its own codes 611, so much of the VAE's edge is a known code distribution; real images score 18.9 (FD in the topic classifier's features) <span class=\"meas\">measured</span> (Latent lab).","beta":"16-number VAE, &beta; 0.1 to 10: KL falls from 43.9 to 3.8 nats, dimensions in use from 16 to 5, reconstruction from 21.4 to 16.4 dB; prior samples are best at &beta; = 0.3 to 1 (FD 694, 689) against 734 at 0.1 and 1,029 at 10 <span class=\"meas\">measured</span> (Latent lab).","mis_scale":"Measured: without the factor the signal-to-noise ratio is about 30 times the trained one at every timestep (20,453 against 679 at the first); image-to-image with factor 1.0 smears (pixel spread 22 against 38), and not dividing before decoding gives a grey fog (spread 15 against 65; a plain round trip falls from 30.9 to 19.0 dB) <span class=\"meas\">measured</span> (Latent lab).","steps":"Tiny DDPM on Fashion-MNIST (FD on 1,000 samples; real images score 18.9): DDPM sampling at 1 / 4 / 10 / 50 / 1,000 steps gives 1,493.9 / 300.3 / 130.5 / 62.3 / 34.9, DDIM at 4 / 10 / 50 gives 478.9 / 181.3 / 112.7. On this briefly trained model DDPM beats DDIM from 4 steps up; \"DDIM is better at few steps\" holds for well-trained models such as the DDIM paper's. On Stable Diffusion v1.5 (DPM-Solver++, guidance 7.5), 1 to 4 steps give a blur, 10 a usable image, 25 and 50 nearly the same one <span class=\"meas\">measured</span> (Diffusion lab).","cfg":"Stable Diffusion v1.5, one lighthouse prompt: the empty prompt draws an unrelated scene, scale 2 and above the lighthouse, past 10 a harsh orange sky (mean saturation 0.35 at 1, 0.47 at 7.5, 0.61 at 20). On the tiny model, class accuracy rises from 77.8% at scale 1 to 96.4% at 5 while diversity falls from 28.38 to 20.28 at 15 <span class=\"meas\">measured</span> (Diffusion lab).","sd_sec_per_pass":0.319,"flow_vs_diff":"Same tiny U-Net, data and 4,000 steps: flow with Euler scores FD 158.3 at 4 steps and 64.4 at 10 against DDIM's 478.9 and 181.3, and beats every diffusion sampler at every step count from 1 to 50 (48.9 against DDPM's 62.3 at 50); but DDPM at 1,000 steps (34.9) still beats any unguided flow sampler, and guided flow reaches 23.8. Reflow takes 1-step FD from 1,253.5 to 159.8 <span class=\"meas\">measured</span> (Flow lab).","vlm_halluc":"SmolVLM answers 26 of 30 known-answer questions at 1,088 tokens (24 of 30 with one 64-token tile). POPE-style probe: yes to 0 of 16 random absent objects, 2 of 31 adversarial ones (a dog in the DOG-sticker photo, a legend on the chart), 24 of 25 present. About 2.1 s per answer, 1.6 s of it in the vision encoder; peak 2,444 MiB <span class=\"meas\">measured</span> (VLM lab, bf16 on MPS).","t_latent":"about 28 min (the tab's own measurement)","t_diff":"about 20 minutes (19.5 at 200 words per minute, measured by check_page.mjs on 2026-10-17), plus about 3 for the folded interview answers and code","t_flow":"about 25 minutes (25.3 at 200 words per minute, measured by check_page.mjs on 2026-10-17), plus about 7 for the folded interview answers and code","t_vision":"about 22 min (the tab's own measurement)","t_vlm":"about 20 min (the tab's own measurement)","t_map":"about 8 to 10 min (the tab's own estimate)"}};
